WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.961
Urban form describes the spatial arrangement of people, jobs, activities, buildings, and networks.

2
00:00:05.961 --> 00:00:13.322
The source chapter examines how concentrated, corridor, polycentric, and dispersed patterns interact with transportation.

3
00:00:13.322 --> 00:00:15.755
Density alone does not determine travel.

4
00:00:15.755 --> 00:00:22.994
The location and mix of destinations, street connectivity, transit quality, parking, and regional structure all matter.

5
00:00:22.994 --> 00:00:28.530
Agglomeration can increase productivity and access by bringing related activities together.

6
00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.328
It can also increase land cost and congestion.

7
00:00:31.328 --> 00:00:39.784
A polycentric region may shorten some trips by distributing employment, yet generate complex cross-travel if housing and jobs do not match.

8
00:00:39.784 --> 00:00:45.380
Network design shapes which of those destinations are practical by walking, transit, or car.

9
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:49.578
Job accessibility is more informative than the number of jobs nearby.

10
00:00:49.578 --> 00:00:54.992
It combines travel impedance, competition, mode availability, and worker characteristics.

11
00:00:54.992 --> 00:01:04.664
A job may be geographically close but inaccessible because the shift begins before transit service, the route is unsafe, or the worker lacks the required mode.

12
00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.645
Models should therefore represent several scales.

13
00:01:07.645 --> 00:01:11.112
Neighborhood form affects local trips and station access.

14
00:01:11.112 --> 00:01:14.640
Regional form affects employment and long-distance travel.

15
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:21.210
Equity analysis asks whether access differs by income, education, race, disability, or vehicle availability.

16
00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:22.517
Torrance application.

17
00:01:22.517 --> 00:01:28.055
This separate section cites the Torrance General Plan and Torrance Transit Tomorrow Plan.

18
00:01:28.055 --> 00:01:36.828
Torrance has major employment, residential, commercial, and industrial areas, while its transit routes extend into neighboring jurisdictions.

19
00:01:36.828 --> 00:01:41.060
A local accessibility analysis should not stop at the city boundary.

20
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:48.091
A conceptual study could map jobs reachable within selected travel times by car, transit, walking, and bicycling.

21
00:01:48.091 --> 00:01:56.927
It should use service by time of day, account for transfers and first-and-last-mile conditions, and distinguish job categories where possible.

22
00:01:56.927 --> 00:02:03.772
Housing cost and worker characteristics would help reveal whether proximity translates into a realistic match.

23
00:02:03.772 --> 00:02:09.870
Scenario comparisons could test a land-use change, a frequency improvement, or a safer connection.

24
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:17.897
The result should report both regional access and local burdens such as truck conflict, displacement risk, or increased exposure.

25
00:02:17.897 --> 00:02:19.950
No such model is calculated here.

26
00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:28.475
The application shows why urban form, service, and the labor market must be interpreted together rather than reduced to a density target.

27
00:02:28.475 --> 00:02:36.751
